    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d157">

                <pb facs="0397" n="375" sameAs="frus:frus1950v05/pg375"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">781.00/5–1850: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Embassy in Greece
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-05-18T13:00:00-04:00">May 18, 1950—1 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>901. Immed fol tel contains message,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d157-f1"/> approved by
                        Grady,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d157-f2"/> from McGhee to be delivered Venizelos
                    in reply his ltr Apr 26 (Deptel 837 May
                        8)<anchor n="3" xml:id="d157-f3"/> unless you perceive some objection. Dept
                    has no objection to publication message by Venizelos if he desires.</p>
                <p>Reply drafted in light urtel 1065 May 10<anchor
                        n="4" xml:id="d157-f4"/> and several other messages re unsatisfactory
                    relations with Venizelos, and attempts strike balance between giving Venizelos
                    excessive and unwarranted encouragement on one hand and, on other, alienating
                    him to extent that Liberal Party support might be withdrawn from Govt with
                    disastrous consequences.</p>
                <p>As result Venizelos’s capriciousness and manifest incompetence and his feud with
                    Papandreou, we are coming to question whether it desirable press him accept
                    important post of FonMin where he wld be
                    hopelessly weak rep of Greece and probably cause daily difficulties. <pb
                        facs="0398" n="376" xml:id="pg376"/> It might well be preferable, if at all
                    feasible, that this post be taken by another Liberal of greater stability and
                    talent with Venizelos occupying merely titular position of Deputy PriMin. In fact best solution wld seem removal
                    of Venizelos as Liberal Leader by his own Party. If you agree and if you see any
                    possibility work towards this objective without undue danger to present Govt,
                    presume you will do so.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Webb</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
